BODY = '''<h2>What the Public Sources Say</h2>
<p>MedlinePlus, in its "Bathroom safety for adults" page, says raising the toilet seat height can help prevent falls, either with an raised seat or with a commode chair used in place of a toilet. It does not name a safety frame. The CDC's "Check for Safety" checklist suggests grab bars next to the toilet, and a frame is a grab bar on legs. The ADA Standards put a horizontal toilet grab bar 33 to 36 inches above the floor and test it for 250 pounds of force, and the Medline, Vaunn and HOMLAND height ranges all reach into that band.</p>
<p>We found no standard that covers toilet safety frames, so every weight rating in this guide is the seller's own figure. Treat it as an upper limit and not as a test result.</p>
<p class="note"><strong>Ask a professional if you are unsure.</strong> A doctor or occupational therapist can tell you whether a frame, a raised seat or a bedside commode suits you, and a physical therapist can show you how to stand safely.</p>

<h2>How to Choose a Toilet Safety Frame</h2>
<h3>Free-standing or bolted</h3>
<p>A free-standing frame, such as the Vaunn, rests on the floor and needs no fitting, so it suits a rental or a shared bathroom and can move to another toilet. It relies on its feet and your weight to stay put. A bolted frame, such as the HOMLAND or Vive, is held by the seat screws, so it cannot slide, but it only fits toilets with the right bolt spacing and tank clearance.</p>
<h3>Check the fit</h3>
<p>Measure the width of your toilet, the distance between the seat bolts and the gap to the tank. The HOMLAND listing asks for 5.5-inch bolt spacing and a 3-inch base. The Vive listing asks for 2 inches between the bolt holes and the tank and excludes curved tanks, bidet seats and quick-release hinges. The Carex width is 16 to 18 inches, so check it against the bowl.</p>
<h3>Weight rating</h3>
<p>The five listings range from 250 to 450 pounds. Pick a rating well above your weight, since you push down on the arms with force when you stand. Our <a href="/tools/weight-limit-finder/">weight limit finder</a> filters the picks in our guides by the load you need.</p>
<h3>Height and width</h3>
<p>The arms should sit near your hip height when you stand, so you can push without leaning. Frames that adjust over a wide range, like the Vaunn at 26 to 34 inches, suit more people. If you are using a raised seat, add its height first. Our <a href="/tools/raised-toilet-seat-height-calculator/">raised toilet seat calculator</a> can help.</p>
<h3>Frame or raised seat</h3>
<p>A frame helps with pushing and a raised seat reduces how far you must lower and lift. Someone with weak legs may use both. If the toilet is too low, start with a raised seat, and see our <a href="/best-raised-toilet-seats/">raised toilet seat guide</a>. If you need a toilet beside the bed, see the <a href="/best-bedside-commodes/">bedside commode guide</a>.</p>

<h2>Toilet Safety Frame Questions</h2>
<h3>What is a toilet safety frame?</h3>
<p>It is a pair of padded armrests on legs that stands around the toilet or is bolted to it, so you can push down on both sides to sit or stand. It does not raise the seat, and it does not replace a grab bar fixed to the wall, but it needs no drilling.</p>
<h3>Do toilet safety rails fit every toilet?</h3>
<p>No. The HOMLAND listing asks for two-hole toilets with 5.5-inch bolt spacing and a base at least 3 inches deep, and the Vive listing excludes curved tanks, smart and bidet toilets and quick-release seats. Free-standing frames fit more toilets, but measure the width of yours before you buy.</p>
<h3>Is a free-standing frame safe?</h3>
<p>It can be, if the floor is firm and dry and the feet grip, since the frame stays put through your weight and its feet. The Vaunn listing describes suction feet that work on tile, vinyl and hardwood. Push on each arm before first use to check that it does not slide, and stop using it if it does.</p>
<h3>How much weight can they hold?</h3>
<p>The listings give 250 pounds for the Medline, 300 for the Carex and Vaunn, 350 for the HOMLAND and 450 for the Vive. These are the sellers' figures, and we found no standard or test that backs them. Choose a rating well above your weight.</p>
<h3>Can I use one with a raised toilet seat?</h3>
<p>Yes, with a check. The Medline listing says it adjusts to fit standard or raised seats. A raised seat changes the height of the seat and the gap to the tank, so confirm that a bolted frame still fits, and adjust the arm height to your new seat level.</p>
<h3>Does Medicare cover a toilet safety frame?</h3>
<p>We could not confirm that it does. Medicare.gov's page on durable medical equipment does not mention toilet safety frames, and University of Utah Health says many aids are not typically covered by insurance. Ask your plan and your doctor before you buy, and keep the receipt in case you need it.</p>

<h2>Our Take</h2>
<p>For a free-standing frame, the Vaunn gives the most information and the widest adjustment, with 4,458 reviews at 4.6. For a bolted one, the HOMLAND has a 350-pound rating and a clear fit list. The Medline has by far the most reviews, 25,272 at 4.4, but a 250-pound rating.</p>
<p>The Vive suits a shared bathroom or a heavier user, if your toilet is on its list. The Carex is the plainest and has the lowest rating, and its listing leaves out the height.</p>

<h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
<p>The Vaunn is the best free-standing frame and the HOMLAND the best bolt-on. Choose the Vive for flip-up arms and a 450-pound rating, and the Medline for the longest record. Measure your toilet first, and ask a therapist if you are unsure which aid you need.</p>'''

